## Lore

The Anti-Robot Specialist, called a Robot Slammer or just a Slammer, is a
Headhunter who has turned a deep mechanical aptitude toward one purpose:
finding, countering and wrecking robots and power armor. Slammers study
computers, exoskeletons, robot vehicles and artificial intelligences with a
devotion that borders on obsession, memorizing designs, statistics and field
performance, and chasing every rumor of a new model or upgrade.

It would be easy to mistake them for robotics hobbyists with guns. They are
also expert combat pilots who can wring the last bit of performance out of a
machine, and they love robot-against-robot fighting the way a racer loves the
track. Most operate a stolen suit of power armor or a robot vehicle, and
taking a Coalition machine is counted a mark of real nerve. Glitter Boys are
generally passed over as too slow and limited, though knowing how to beat one
is a point of pride, and one reason a wary Coalition has hired Slammers
against Free Quebec.

Unlike an Operator, who builds and repairs, the Slammer learns a machine in
order to defeat it: blind spots, weak joints, payloads and the tactics its
owners favor. All are literate, and most learn Techno-Can as a second tongue.
Oddly, simple vehicles such as hovercycles and combustion engines hold little
interest for them. In the field they favor weapons that hit hard: explosives,
rail guns, mini-missiles and particle beams.

## GM Notes

Fanatic Robophile: -25% for uncommon, foreign or alien robots and power armor,
-40% for extremely rare, alien or never before seen ones. A failed roll means
a missed fault, a misjudgment or a forgotten detail; the G.M. may ask for a
roll whenever the information matters under pressure. It adds +5% to Robot
Electronics, Robot Mechanics, Weapon Systems and Computer Repair (the last
two only for systems used in robots and power armor). Other O.C.C.s
(Techno-Hound, Operator, CS Technical Officer at the cost of two MOS skills,
Scientist and Mechanic types) may take it only as an O.C.C. Related skill, at
50% +3% per level with no O.C.C. bonus, and their other mechanical and
electronics bonuses are halved.

Not applied on the sheet: immunity to Horror Factor caused by mechanical
looking opponents, power armor, cyborgs and robots; saves at -2 against the
Horror Factor of magic and the supernatural; +2 initiative against robots and
power armor only. Psychic Slammers save at +2 against Horror Factor and
possession; a Partial Borg psychic has psionic range, damage and duration halved, I.S.P.
costs up 50%, and all O.C.C. skill bonuses halved.

Starting kit beyond what the sheet lists: one weapon for each W.P. with three
full reloads (often Triax or Bandito Arms), a single personalized suit of
environmental armor, light or medium, with a communicator built in, two sets of
street clothes, a black paint stick, an Automatic Lock Pick/Release Gun, a
lock pick kit, Electro-Adhesive Pads, a box of 100 disposable plastic gloves
and personal items. Many also pick up electronic bugs and other surveillance
gear. The Plasma grenades may be taken as the equivalent in plastique.

Roll or assign the Mode of Transportation (see the ability of that name).

Money: besides the credits, another 1D6x1000 credits in black market items.

Cybernetics: one Multi-Optic Eye, a Universal Headjack & Ear Implant and two
implants of choice.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'headhunter-anti-robot-specialist');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'headhunter-anti-robot-specialist';

